d the first-raise rule. PROTOCOL.md:696-730 documents claim dispositions, the sweep record, the packet field and closed-scope/epoch carrying. commands/pr-review.md explains how to write fixed and disputed claims. Receipt 4 (`diff -r` of the source and desktop protocol trees) exited 0. desktop-projection.json was restaged: the round-policy.js entry changed from 669fa6… (size 7981) to ca022f… (size 8325), and protocolHash changed.","id":"R7","outcome":"PASS"}],"dimensions":[{"evidence":"The epoch fix is correct (round-policy.js:55, execution.js:991, sweep-records.js:99). Claim validation, record construction (classId from retained history), the swept-class predicate and reuse keying (execution.js:1027) are sound, and blocking flags are never rewritten. Two medium defects remain in review-findings.js:48-57/80. The round key merges separate executions on one revision. As a result, a present mark from an earlier execution blocks a later dispute re-review's closure (CR2-1), and a same-group re-report is not counted, so closure depends on storage order (CR2-2).","name":"code","outcome":"FAIL"},{"evidence":"R1-R7 meet their rubric test conditions (see criteria). AC6 (spec.md:77-81) requires a re-report of an earlier finding ID to count as present 'in either processing order'. CR2-2 violates this in a same-revision re-review, and PROTOCOL.md:334-336 ('a disagreement keeps its regression status') is likewise not met there. Decisions D1-D5, D7 and S5-S8 match the code. D6/DEC-4's (scope, round, revision) grouping combined with S10's first-raise rule is the source of CR2-1 and CR2-2. That decision is reversible and within scope, but its interaction with same-revision re-reviews was not reconciled. No repository project rules beyond the spec's Definition of Done apply. The mirror is identical and the CLI suite is planned in CI.","name":"requirements","outcome":"FAIL"},{"evidence":"The consumed Verification (evidence/9de793c7…, runner 6a6a7d61…) has seven receipts on e810f4df, all exit 0. Receipt 0 is the focused sweep-records and round-policy suite (53/53 pass). Receipts 1-2 are the affected CLI suites driving gate.execute and review.execute. Receipt 3 is plugin validate, receipt 4 the mirror diff, receipt 5 the desktop npm test with stage:protocol, and receipt 6 the --claims help check. Every changed source area (protocol modules, the CLI help, the desktop mirror and projection) and every affected suite has a receipt. ciRequiredChecks (Ubuntu 22.04/24.04 acceptance = plugin-ci.yml:93-112 running ci/portable-acceptance.sh with the full CLI suite including host-dependent files; Desktop contract on Ubuntu 24.04 = :142-160, `npm run check --prefix apps/desktop`) cover the approved scope after convergence. The implementer's local alternative is adequate for this round. Caveat: there is no test for the CR2-1/CR2-2 scenarios.","name":"evidence","outcome":"PASS"},{"evidence":"Changes are confined to sweep-records.js; a small round-policy.js addition (scopeEpoch); review-findings.js (#134); small call sites in execution.js (checkClaims, roundPolicy epoch and revision, sweepRecords, the reuse key, the re-report retention) and boundary-review.js (checkClaims, roundSchedule sweeps); the CLI help text; PROTOCOL.md; pr-review.md; the desktop restage and manifest; and tests and fixtures. local-agent-adapters.js is untouched. All changed paths and their interactions were covered.","name":"scope","outcome":"PASS"},{"evidence":"Of the required dispositions, closed-scope-sweep-reuse, desktop-stage-receipt, claims-help-receipt, CR-1 and CR-3 are all resolved. There are two new medium findings, CR2-1 and CR2-2, in the round-group-spans-executions class. Both are on regressionPaths (review-findings.js) and block in round 2 under the all-non-low band.","name":"findings","outcome":"FAIL"}],"dispositions":[{"evidence":"round-policy.js:55 scopeEpoch counts the scope's closed entries in reviewScopeHistory. closeScope and resetRounds (:28-37) push entries on split, integration and amendment. execution.js:991 records epoch in roundPolicy from the pre-count state, and the count itself never changes the epoch. sweep-records.js:99 requires input.roundPolicy.epoch === the current epoch. A later scope with the same name and revision therefore has epoch+1, and old inputs are excluded. This is tested at sweep-records.test.js:144-147 and passes in receipt 0. roundSchedule (boundary-review.js:42) uses the same collection.","id":"codex/review/closed-scope-sweep-reuse","status":"resolved"},{"evidence":"Receipt 5 (`npm test --prefix apps/desktop`, source e810f4df, exit 0) runs `npm run stage:protocol` (stdout lines 3-7) and passes 'Desktop stages the exact protocol and trusted waiver guard…' (line 169), along with the projection and allowlist tests.","id":"codex/review/desktop-stage-receipt","status":"resolved"},{"evidence":"Receipt 6 runs `node cli/bin/workflow.js evaluate --help` piped to grep -q for 'disposition (fixed or disputed) and, for fixed, a sweep record' and exits 0 on source e810f4df.","id":"codex/review/claims-help-receipt","status":"resolved"},{"evidence":"The recorded trigger was that the prepass added a report's own first raise to its round's present set. That no longer happens: review-findings.js:54-57 adds an ID only when an earlier, different round key raised it. round-policy.test.js:553-581 (receipt 0, line 37) shows a same-revision re-review closing a finding its round raised, and round 2 carrying the closure forward with a PASS and no qualification. Other instances of the same grouping class remain, reported as new findings CR2-1 and CR2-2.","id":"claude-code/CR-1","status":"resolved"},{"evidence":"Receipt 5 now runs the full desktop test suite on this candidate tree (exit 0), including protocol staging and the projection test.","id":"claude-code/CR-3","status":"resolved"}],"featureId":"tb-convergence-sweep-records","findingProvenance":[{"id":"claude-code/CR2-1","model":"claude-opus-5-5","originalId":"CR2-1","provider":"claude-code"},{"id":"claude-code/CR2-2","model":"claude-opus-5-5","originalId":"CR2-2","provider":"claude-code"}],"findings":[{"blocking":true,"classId":"round-group-spans-executions","classStatement":"Grouping by (scope, epoch, round, revision) merges separate executions of one revision, so a present mark from an earlier execution stops a later same-revision re-review from closing the finding.","criterion":"gate:review","evidence":"review-findings.js:48 builds the key from policy.scope, epoch, round and revision only. :53 adds every `present` disposition to that key's set. :56 adds a re-report whose ID was raised under another key. :80 skips resolved/unsupported when the key's set contains the ID. round-policy.js:26 does not add a round for a repeated revision, and :65 gives that revision the same round, so execution.js:986/991 records an identical key for the re-review. :92-94 accept only resolved/unsupported/present/present-low, not a carry-forward. Base review-findings.js:39 keyed by input.reviewRound, which differs per execution, so the later closure applied. The round-1 fix (raisedIn) removes only the first-raise trigger. round-policy.test.js:553-581 tests only a finding raised in the same round group, not one kept present by an earlier execution of that round.","failureScenario":"Codex raises F in round 1. In round 2 (new revision R2), codex re-reports F and/or claude marks it `present`, and the pair FAILs. The implementer sends a disputed claim and re-runs review.execute on unchanged R2, which stays round 2. Both reviewers mark F unsupported and the re-review PASSes. In round 3, execution.js treats F as a reconsider closure and the reviewers validly carry it forward without a disposition. findingObligations then gives every round-2 report the key round:slice:0:2:R2, presentInRound for that key contains F from the first round-2 execution, both unsupported closures are skipped, and F is unreconciled. The round-3 pair is qualified as needing follow-up reconciliation (UNAVAILABLE) even though both reviewers accepted the rebuttal.","id":"claude-code/CR2-1","path":"repository/plugin-src/shared/resources/protocol/current/review-findings.js","severity":"medium"},{"blocking":true,"classId":"round-group-spans-executions","classStatement":"Inside one round group, a re-report from a later execution is not counted as present, so a peer's closure in that round wins or loses depending on result order.","criterion":"gate:review","evidence":"review-findings.js:54-57 track raisedIn per round key, not per raising report: `else if (raisedIn.get(finding.id) !== key) present.add(finding.id)`, so a second report under the same key never marks it present. :80-83 then close F and set origin null when the closing report comes later in the sorted history (:31-32). :75 restores the origin only if the re-report is processed after the closure. AC6 (spec.md:77-81) requires a re-report of an earlier finding ID to count as present 'in either processing order'. No test in round-policy.test.js:504-581 covers a peer disagreement in a same-revision re-review.","failureScenario":"Round 4 codex raises medium regression F on a path the round-4 fix changed. The implementer disputes it and re-runs review.execute on the same revision (still round 4). Codex re-reports F as a finding, claude marks it unsupported, and claude's result is stored after codex's. The re-review FAILs on codex's finding. Round 5 changes another file and codex reports F again with every row passing. Because raisedIn[F] equals the round-4 key, codex's re-report is not counted as present, so claude's unsupported closes F and clears its origin (resolved=true, origin null). In round 5, F is judged only against the round-5 paths and is demoted as a non-regression, so the round-5 pair can PASS while a medium regression of the previous fix is still reported. With the opposite storage order, the origin is restored. This contradicts AC6's 'in either processing order' and PROTOCOL.md:334-336 ('a disagreement keeps its regression status').","id":"claude-code/CR2-2","path":"repository/plugin-src/shared/resources/protocol/current/review-findings.js","severity":"medium"}],"gate":"review","historyAssessment":null,"input":{"bytes":20395,"format":"json","hash":"sha256:658c3f8e67fce30fe89b9b4439bdef35f18006831f23b58aa633c72ec7f30c9f"},"kind":"result","mandateAssessment":{"evidence":"The goal (close #144 with cross-round regression tracking) is implemented: sweep records, packet carrying with a scope epoch, the swept-class regression in roundSchedule, and reviewer guidance. #134 is folded in with its rationale recorded (D6, S5, DEC-4). The execution.js and boundary-review.js edits stay small, and local-agent-adapters.js is unchanged. The record format is versioned 'sweep-record/1' for #148. Agent decisions are recorded with alternatives and reversibility, and none makes an irreversible human-level choice. There is no merge evidence. The CR2 defects are correctness gaps, not mandate violations. The PR description and readiness are not in the pinned view; that is a caveat, not a violation.","outcome":"PASS"},"mode":"full","outcome":"FAIL","prior":null,"priorCandidate":null,"receipt":{"bytes":745,"format":"json","hash":"sha256:daefd66e78fb87dee5260571d8c2b1121a9046b38ca233e84bb6ca47494c2e13"},"reviewerLaunched":true,"runId":"tb-convergence-sweep-records-1","summary":"Round-2 feature review of tb-convergence-sweep-records at head e810f4df (base and target 6874b9d6). The three round-1 fixes I was asked to check hold up in the source. (1) A scope epoch closes the closed-scope reuse hole: round-policy.js:55 computes scopeEpoch, execution.js:991 records it in the packet's roundPolicy, and sweep-records.js:99 requires it to match; sweep-records.test.js:144-147 tests it. (2) CR-1's first-raise trigger is fixed: review-findings.js:54-57 adds raisedIn, tested at round-policy.test.js:553-581. (3) The desktop and help-text receipts now exist (receipts 5 and 6). However, the #134 fold-in still groups every execution on one revision into a single round key: (scope, epoch, round, revision) at review-findings.js:48. Because a same-revision re-review keeps the same round (round-policy.js:26, :57, :65), the key mixes separate executions, and that leaves two blocking medium defects in the same class. (a) If an earlier execution in the round kept a finding present (a `present` disposition, or a re-report of an ID raised in an earlier round), a later same-revision dispute re-review cannot close it, even when both reviewers mark it unsupported. The next round then sees an unreconciled finding, the same failure CR-1 described. (b) If the round that first raised a finding is re-reviewed and one peer re-reports it while the other marks it unsupported, the re-report is not counted as present. Whether the finding closes then depends on the order the results were stored, which contradicts AC6's 'in either processing order'. In rounds 4-5 it can also clear a medium regression's origin, so the regression is wrongly demoted. All rubric criteria R1-R7 meet their stated test conditions, and evidence and scope are complete. Caveats: the PR description and status are not in the pinned view. CR-2 (low) was not required for disposition and is unchanged by the implementer's choice."}